@@ -0,0 +1,1591 @@
+# Glossary (agent-readable view)
+
+Generated by `node glossary-query.ts agent` from the term records and usage.json. Do not edit by hand.
+
+How to use: write with these labels exactly. Before using a word that looks like a term, `get` it; if its meaning is not what you mean, use the term named under Instead. A term with no usage note is not yet safe to use without reading its properties.
+
+## account
+
+A named party a venture keeps a record for, at accounts/<id>/account.json, with a relationship: client, customer, user, member, partner or other. Optional; who a product serves in general is its market
+
+- id: `noun:account`, level: noun/venture, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard CRM usage (Salesforce, HubSpot: account)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## agent harness
+
+An AI harness in which the model directs its own steps and tool use in a loop toward a goal, acting on files, tools and systems between the person's turns, under permissions (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Hermes Agent). The default kind of interface. Every agent harness is an AI harness; a chatbot is not an agent harness
+
+- id: `noun:agent-harness`, level: noun/interface, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard agent harness (industry usage)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## agent-readable glossary
+
+A glossary whose every entry also says how and when to use the term, so an agent (or a new person) can write with it correctly.
+
+- id: `agent-glossary`, level: reference, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
+- Must hold: E1 its entries are terms (words or phrases); E2 each entry says what its term means; GL1 limited to one subject field, work or venture; AG1 each entry says how and when to use the term
+- Use for: the glossary view in which every entry also has a usage note; generated, never edited by hand
+- Not for: a second copy of the glossary kept by hand
+- Related: dictionary (is_a), glossary (is_a), index (overlap), registry (overlap), thesaurus (overlap)
+
+## AI feature
+
+A model used inside a product that does its job without it (summaries, search, drafting). Test: remove the model and the product still works. Most apps that implement AI are here, not harnesses
+
+- id: `noun:ai-feature`, level: noun/interface, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard common product usage
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## AI harness
+
+A product that is the software around a model: the person's work happens in exchanges with it, so without the model nothing is left. A chatbot is one. The model answers; the person acts
+
+- id: `noun:ai-harness`, level: noun/interface, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard harness (industry usage)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## ambition
+
+Why the venture exists and how far it means to go: hobby, survival, lifestyle, managed growth, aggressive growth
+
+- id: `noun:ambition`, level: noun/venture, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard IRS (hobby); venture-type literature
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## artifact
+
+A view made for people to read, show or share: a page, doc, deck or report. The only thing a venture publishes; records stay private.
+
+- id: `artifact`, level: data, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
+- Must hold: VW1 derived from records; VW2 can be rebuilt from its records; VW3 not a source of truth; AR1 made for people
+- Use for: a view made for people (page, doc, deck, report); the only thing a venture publishes
+- Not for: records, or anything edited as the source of truth
+- Instead: the source of truth -> record@data
+- Related: view (is_a)
+
+## board
+
+A formal board of directors (or managers, or trustees), which holds legal authority, or an advisory board, which advises and decides nothing (governance/board.json)
+
+- id: `noun:board`, level: noun/governance, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard corporate law; Feld and Ramsinghani, Startup Boards
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## bottleneck
+
+An open problem that keeps coming back or holds other problems open, so the venture's throughput is limited by it.
+
+- id: `bottleneck`, level: problem, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
+- Must hold: PR1 a gap between a present and a wanted state; PR2 someone has it; BN1 open; BN2 came back at least twice or holds at least two problems open
+- Use for: an open problem that recurs or holds other problems open, limiting throughput
+- Not for: any hard or slow problem
+- Related: problem (is_a), root-problem (overlap), function-problem (overlap), technical-problem (overlap), decision (overlap)
+
+## brand guidelines
+
+The rules for using the venture's visual and verbal identity, kept by the brand pillar
+
+- id: `noun:brand-guidelines`, level: noun/governance, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard brand guidelines or brand book (common marketing usage)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## business model
+
+How a product earns, by St. Gallen pattern names
+
+- id: `noun:business-model`, level: noun/purpose, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard St. Gallen Business Model Navigator
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## cap table
+
+The record of who owns the venture and on what terms: share classes, holders, grants with vesting, the option pool, SAFEs and notes (governance/ownership.json)
+
+- id: `noun:cap-table`, level: noun/governance, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard capitalization table (common startup and finance usage)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## capability register
+
+What agents can, cannot, may not or are not yet known to do, task by task, with sources and dates, fed by research and our own evals (evidence/capabilities/agent-capabilities.json, EE-029)
+
+- id: `noun:capability-register`, level: noun/evidence, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard ours
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## case generator
+
+A generator whose output is test cases from a seed, so a check runs over many cases and reproduces.
+
+- id: `case-generator`, level: module, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
+- Must hold: G1 produces new instances; G2 what it makes is fixed by a given spec, template, schema or seed; G3 same input, same output; CG1 outputs test cases; CG2 writes nothing to disk
+- Use for: a generator of test cases from a seed, reproducible
+- Not for: fixtures written by hand
+- Related: generator (is_a)
+
+## catalog
+
+A collection of items (books, products, datasets, services), each described by its attributes.
+
+- id: `catalog`, level: reference, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
+- Must hold: CA1 its entries are items; CA2 each entry describes its item by attributes
+- Use for: items (products, datasets, books, tools) described by attributes
+- Not for: terms or people
+- Instead: terms -> glossary; people or services and how to reach them -> directory
+- Related: registry (overlap)
+
+## central data management structure
+
+What a venture's scaffolding is (EE-085): one structure for everything the venture knows, under one set of conventions, with pointers to the systems of record that keep the rest. It suits solo founders and small founding teams, for whom one central structure replaces separate SaaS silos and gives agents the whole picture
+
+- id: `noun:central-data-management-structure`, level: noun/venture, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard master data management, centralized style (DAMA-DMBOK)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## channel
+
+A place the venture publishes to (website, docs site, social account, newsletter, app store, press, investor update, regulatory filing); its destination decides which guidelines apply
+
+- id: `noun:channel`, level: noun/governance, scope: venture, status: ontology new
+- Must hold: standard marketing channel (Kotler, Marketing Management)
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## client
+
+Was the record for a party a venture serves; not universal, since many ventures have customers, users, members or an audience instead. Now a value of an account's relationship (served under a professional relationship)
+
+- id: `noun:client`, level: noun/venture, scope: venture, status: ontology retired
+- Must hold: standard common usage
+- Use for: (no usage note yet)
+
+## Co-founder
+
+A founder of a venture that has at least one other founder.
+
+- id: `co-founder`, level: membership, scope: venture, status: proposed
+- Must hold: F1 a person, or an entity whose owners trace to people; F2 there before the first hire, investment or paying customer; F3 brought a founding contribution, evidenced in provenance; F4 has a stake: equity or a signed founder agreement; F5 shows up in provenance through decisions or work; CF1 another founder on the same venture
+- Use for: a founder of a venture that has another founder; is a founder
+- Not for: an early employee, or a 'sub-founder'
+- Instead: brought no founding contribution -> early-employee
+- Related: founder (is_a), early-employee (overlap)
+
+## Coach
+
+Someone who, under an explicit agreement, helps the client reach the client's own goal mostly by asking, with an accountability loop and sacred trust.
+
+- id: `coach`, level: relationship, scope: venture, status: proposed
+- Must hold: C1 explicit agreement: goal, success, duration; C2 elicitive: most turns are questions or reflections; C3 process, not path (a permission, never fails); C4 commitments made and reviewed; C5 the client owns the goal and actions; C6 sacred trust T1 to T5 hold
+- Use for: someone under explicit agreement who helps the client reach the client's own goal mostly by asking
+- Not for: a mentor
+- Instead: has walked the path -> mentor
+- Related: mentor (overlap), friend (overlap)
+
+## code generator
+
+A generator that writes source code or types derived from a schema, so the code cannot drift from the schema.
+
+- id: `code-generator`, level: module, scope: venture, status: proposed (sandbox)
