+<div class="tblwrap"><table class="vtbl"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Means</th><th>Relation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>software system</td><td>Top level. Delivers value to people on its own and can be deployed. Naive and Mentura are each one.</td><td>root of the containment tree. Disjoint from container, component and module.</td></tr><tr><td>container</td><td>Runs or stores data separately: an app, process, server or store. Part of a software system.</td><td>child of software system. Sibling of nothing at its level. Disjoint from component and module.</td></tr><tr><td>data store</td><td>A container that keeps data.</td><td>is_a container.</td></tr><tr><td>component</td><td>A group of modules behind a contract, inside one container. Can't run alone.</td><td>child of container. Disjoint from module and interface.</td></tr><tr><td>module</td><td>One unit of source code: one file.</td><td>child of component. Overlaps interface, because a schema or types file is both.</td></tr><tr><td>kernel</td><td>A module that 3 or more others depend on, that depends on nothing inside, is small, has no effects and is tested.</td><td>kind of module (module level). Overlaps engine.</td></tr><tr><td>engine</td><td>A module that takes rules as input, returns which failed, is domain-free, deterministic and tested.</td><td>kind of module. Overlaps kernel; the term engine is both.</td></tr><tr><td>interface</td><td>The agreed boundary two elements share: operations, messages, formats. A contract, not a running thing.</td><td>cuts across levels. Overlaps schema. Disjoint from component.</td></tr><tr><td>schema</td><td>A definition of what valid data looks like. It defines a record type.</td><td>kind of contract. Overlaps interface. Disjoint from record.</td></tr><tr><td>record</td><td>One piece of the venture's data, conforming to a schema. The source of truth.</td><td>disjoint from schema.</td></tr><tr><td>framework</td><td>Reusable software that calls the code you plug in (inversion of control).</td><td>describes a software system. Disjoint from library. Overlaps platform.</td></tr><tr><td>library</td><td>Reusable software your code calls; it never calls you.</td><td>disjoint from framework.</td></tr><tr><td>platform</td><td>A hosted software system with accounts, which others build on.</td><td>is_a software system.</td></tr><tr><td>root</td><td>A position, not a kind: the one element with no parent that everything else is part of.</td><td>Lean proves a hierarchy has at most one root. Overlaps software system.</td></tr><tr><td>person</td><td>A human user or owner. An agent is not a person.</td><td>disjoint from every software term.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+<h2>Our conventions, not technical terms</h2>
+<div class="tblwrap"><table class="vtbl"><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>purpose, knowledge, operations, governance, evidence</td><td>Our own <b>areas</b> of a venture (decided 2026-10-31: renamed from 'layers', which now means only the technical sense). They organize what a venture is about. Folders and layer.json keep their names until a later cleanup.</td></tr><tr><td>system (the layer)</td><td>Our name for the shared-records area in <code>schema/layer.json</code>. It is not the software system.</td></tr><tr><td>interface (the folder)</td><td>Holds containers (CLI, MCP server, API) and some core components. Interfaces in the technical sense are the contracts those containers offer.</td></tr><tr><td>schema/ (the folder)</td><td>Holds 165 code files as well as schemas, so the folder name is misleading. The technical term schema means only the data definitions.</td></tr><tr><td>verification (the folder)</td><td>Holds TLA+ models. Verification is the activity of checking (ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207). The models are development containers.</td></tr><tr><td>framework core</td><td>My own label on the last map. It means nothing defined, so it's gone. The parts it covered are now named components.</td></tr><tr><td>platform (the folder)</td><td>Code filed as the platform before EE-097, when Mentura was the platform's name. Which product the platform is now is undecided.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+<div class="note"><h2>What the engine caught</h2><ul>
+<li><b>Software system versus component.</b> My first software-system test let a component pass, so the two overlapped. I added S4: a software system can be run or deployed on its own.</li>
+<li><b>Sampling hid an overlap.</b> The seeded sample never produced a framework that is also a platform, so they looked disjoint. Every fact here is yes or no, so the run now checks every combination.</li>
+<li><b>Interface versus component.</b> Exhaustive enumeration showed my interface test let a group of code pass. I added IF4: an interface is a contract, not a group of code. It also showed that a single file can be an interface, such as a schema file, so module and interface overlap.</li>
+</ul></div>
